  "live_records": {
    "q-111601": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111601",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Factor ${p}^{2}+49+14p.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}+2ap+{a}^{2}=(p+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}+49+14p={p}^{2}+14p+49.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}+14p+{7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}+2\\cdot 7p+{7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=7.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+7)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
