    "q-111618": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111618",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Factor the expression ${n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2an+{a}^{2}=(n+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+\\frac{25}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}n+{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{5}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(n+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
