    "q-111620": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111620",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Factor ${p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2ap+{a}^{2}=(p-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}p+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(p-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
